        },
        "title": "Type of Renewable Energy Technologies",
        "cta": null,
        "intro": "M8: Renewable Energy Technologies and Innovations"
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Lesson 4 Overview</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "This lesson introduces the types of renewable energy technologies, including variable sources like solar and wind, dispatchable options such as bioenergy, centralized systems like hydropower, and distributed systems such as rooftop solar. Further details about each renewable energy source will be provided in the next chapter."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "key_concepts",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "title": "Key Concepts",
        "intro": "Explore foundational ideas from this lesson.",
        "concepts": []
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Renewable energy technologies are at the forefront of the global energy transition They offer a broad spectrum of solutions tailored to diverse needs and applications. From rooftop solar panels that power individual households to large-scale wind farms and advanced biofuels for industrial use, these technologies are reshaping how we produce and consume energy. Their versatility enables them to meet a range of energy demands, including electricity generation, transportation, heating, and cooking.<br /><br />A key feature of renewable energy technologies is their adaptability to specific conditions and purposes. These technologies can be classified in several ways:"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_8/2551_192.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Variability in Energy Output</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "A renewable energy source that fluctuates within a relatively short time frame, such as wind and solar energy, which vary within daily, hourly and even sub- hourly time frames. By contrast, resources and technologies that are variable on an annual or seasonal basis due to environmental changes, such as hydropower (due to changes in rainfall) and thermal power plants (due to changes in temperature of ambient air and cooling water), do not fall into this category."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Variability in Energy Output</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Dispatchable generation refers to sources of electricity that can be programmed on demand at the request of power grid operators, according to market needs. Dispatchable generators may adjust their power output according to an order."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_vertical",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_5/M5_Intro_5.png",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_8/2591_4.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Scale of Deployment</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle_small",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Centralized Systems</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Large-scale installations like hydropower plants supply electricity to extensive areas through centralized grids."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle_small",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Distributed Systems</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Decentralized solutions, including rooftop solar panels and small wind turbines, provide energy directly to local users, enhancing energy access in remote regions."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_vertical",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_5/M5_C2_L2_20.png",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_8/2591_22.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Method of Energy Conversion</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle_small",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Direct Use</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Technologies like solar thermal systems capture sunlight to produce heat directly."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle_small",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Indirect Use</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Systems such as solar PV panels convert sunlight into electricity, which can then power various applications."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
